{"findings":[{"path":"packages/excalidraw/components/App.tsx","symbol":"insertImages","line":10704,"explanation":"Batch image-insert editor method. It creates a pending image stand-in per file via newImagePlaceholder, lays the batch out with positionElementsOnGrid, inserts the placeholders, then awaits initializeImage for every file in parallel through Promise.all. Any per-file rejection is caught: state.errorMessage is set and that placeholder is returned as a deleted element. After initialization it re-runs positionElementsOnGrid only over surviving elements, maps the re-positioned survivors and the original/initialized/deleted elements back into the scene, then issues one updateScene with selectedElementIds containing only the survivors and CaptureUpdateAction.IMMEDIATELY, followed by actionFinalize."},{"path":"packages/element/src/positionElementsOnGrid.ts","symbol":"positionElementsOnGrid","line":7,"explanation":"Grid layout routine. It accepts either a flat element list or atomic groups, treats each element/group as a unit, chooses ceil(sqrt(numberOfUnits)) columns for a roughly square layout, groups units into rows, measures each unit with getCommonBounds, and computes row widths, row maximum heights, inter-unit and inter-row padding. It centres the whole grid on centerX/centerY and returns new elements shifted by newElementWith rather than mutating the inputs. insertImages uses it first for placeholders and again for the non-deleted initialized survivors so failed items do not leave holes."},{"path":"packages/excalidraw/data/blob.ts","symbol":"normalizeFile","line":522,"explanation":"Upload type fixer. It immediately returns if the file already carries the normalizedFileSymbol, preventing repeated work. Files ending in .excalidrawlib or .excalidraw are force-relabelled by suffix to the library or excalidraw MIME type. Otherwise, when the type is missing or starts with image/, it calls getActualMimeTypeFromImage and, if the detected MIME differs from the declared type, replaces the File with one carrying the corrected type (same name). It then stamps normalizedFileSymbol on the returned file. Because the .excalidraw branch runs first, scene files are not content-sniffed."},{"path":"packages/excalidraw/data/blob.ts","symbol":"getActualMimeTypeFromImage","line":474,"explanation":"Magic-number sniffer. It reads only the first 15 bytes through blobToArrayBuffer, renders them as a space-separated decimal string, and matches leading-byte regexes for PNG (137 80 78 71...), JPEG (255 216 255), GIF (71 73 70 56 57 97), and WebP (RIFF....WEBP/VP8). On a match it returns the corresponding MIME type; otherwise it falls back to the file's declared type or null. This is what reclassifies a WebP whose filename lies with a .png extension."},{"path":"packages/excalidraw/components/App.tsx","symbol":"initializeImage","line":10390,"explanation":"Per-upload initializer. It rejects unsupported types, sets the wait cursor, and for SVG parses/sanitizes the markup with normalizeSVG and wraps it back into an SVG File (throwing errors.svgImageInsertError if invalid). It derives fileId from the generateIdForFile prop or generateIdFromFile on the current bytes before any resizing. It checks this.files[fileId]: only when no existing dataURL exists does it attempt resizeImageFile with imageOptions (errors are caught and logged, not fatal) and enforce maxFileSizeBytes, throwing fileTooBig if still over. It reuses an existing dataURL or creates one with getDataURL, registers the asset through addMissingFiles, populates/awaits the image cache using the editor updateImageCache, throws if that reports erroredFiles, then awaits the cached HTMLImageElement. If an image is available and the stand-in is not the current active newElement, it re-reads the latest stand-in and applies getImageNaturalDimensions before resolving."},{"path":"packages/excalidraw/data/blob.ts","symbol":"resizeImageFile","line":359,"explanation":"Image downscaler. It returns SVG files untouched, rejects unsupported image types with cause UNSUPPORTED, and when no output-type conversion is requested it measures the file and returns it unchanged if its larger dimension already fits maxWidthOrHeight. Otherwise it lazily imports pica and image-blob-reduce in parallel, configures pica with js/wasm features, optionally overrides blob creation for a requested output type at quality 0.8, calls reduce.toBlob with the maximum dimension and alpha, and returns a new File with the same name and the output or original MIME type."},{"path":"packages/element/src/image.ts","symbol":"updateImageCache","line":41,"explanation":"Package-level image-cache routine. For each unique fileId present in files, it rejects binary MIME types, creates an HTMLImageElement promise via loadHTMLImageElement, and immediately stores {image: imagePromise, mimeType} in imageCache before awaiting it, so other callers can observe the in-flight initialization. After the image resolves it replaces the cache entry with the resolved element; failures are recorded in erroredFiles. It returns imageCache, updatedFiles, and erroredFiles. This is distinct from the App.tsx wrapper at line 10603, which reacts to erroredFiles by marking image elements with status error."},{"path":"packages/excalidraw/components/App.tsx","symbol":"getImageNaturalDimensions","line":10573,"explanation":"Natural-size helper. It computes minHeight as max(canvas height - 120, 160) and maxHeight as min(minHeight, floor(canvas height * 0.5) / zoom), then takes the smaller of the decoded bitmap's naturalHeight and maxHeight and derives width from naturalWidth/naturalHeight. It recentres that rectangle on the stand-in using the current element's x/y and width/height, and returns x, y, width, height and crop: null. Thus the decoded intrinsic pixel size is fitted under a viewport-relative height cap without moving off the placeholder centre."}],"flow":"Clicking the toolbar picture button leads to onImageToolbarButtonClick (App.tsx:10534), which computes the canvas-centre scene coordinates, invokes fileOpen with multiple:true and the image extensions, and passes the returned File[] to insertImages. fileOpen already calls normalizeFile on each file (data/filesystem.ts:43), and insertImages calls normalizeFile again as a guard; the normalizedFileSymbol makes the second pass a no-op. For the supplied batch: the WebP-with-.png file is sniffed from its leading RIFF/WEBP bytes and relabelled image/webp; the 9000-pixel JPEG is recognized and later downscaled; the small SVG is recognized as vector; the truncated file may retain its declared MIME type but will fail when an HTMLImageElement is created.

insertImages creates a pending, 100/zoom-sized stand-in for every file, arranges the stand-ins with positionElementsOnGrid around the click point, inserts them, and starts all initializeImage calls in parallel. Each initializer sanitizes SVG markup if needed, computes the fileId digest, conditionally resizes and enforces the byte limit, stores the data URL in files, registers it with the image cache, and waits for decode. Package-level updateImageCache stores the in-flight decode promise before awaiting it; the editor wrapper then marks any caches that errored with status error. For a successfully decoded image, initializeImage re-fetches the newest stand-in from the scene (so concurrent moves/edits are not overwritten) and applies the intrinsic dimensions via getImageNaturalDimensions. If any initializer rejects, insertImages records the error message in state and replaces that stand-in with a deleted element, so the undecodable file's placeholder is removed from view and is not selected. insertImages then re-runs positionElementsOnGrid over the non-deleted survivors, rebuilds the element list from the scene using the repositioned/initialized/deleted versions, and commits one undoable updateScene (CaptureUpdateAction.IMMEDIATELY) whose selection contains exactly the survivors; actionFinalize then completes the interaction.

Identity is derived before shrinking because the fileId is a content identity of the original (or, for SVG, sanitized) bytes. Resizing and compression are local policy driven by imageOptions; hashing afterwards would make the same image acquire different IDs on different devices/settings, breaking portability and deduplication. A failed resize is not fatal: initializeImage catches and logs the resize error and continues with the original file, though the subsequent byte-limit check can still reject it as fileTooBig. The byte limit is enforced only inside the !existingFileData?.dataURL branch, i.e. for assets not already stored in this.files, and after the optional resize attempt but before registering/decoding the asset. For an undecodable file, the stand-in is marked isDeleted in insertImages' catch, the user sees the configured error message, and it is excluded from re-layout and final selection (the editor cache wrapper may also have marked its status error first, but the final scene update uses the deleted version). The latest stand-in is re-read because initialization is asynchronous and the user may have moved, resized, or otherwise edited the placeholder meanwhile; using scene.getElement(id) preserves those changes while attaching the new fileId. Intrinsic dimensions are skipped when no decoded imageHTML exists, or when the stand-in is still the active state.newElement (the image is still being placed), so in-progress user sizing is not overwritten. Content sniffing does not run for a file named *.excalidraw: normalizeFile's .excalidraw/.excalidrawlib suffix branches force the scene/library MIME type before the image-or-missing-type branch that calls the magic-number sniffer."}

{"findings":[{"path":"excalidraw-app/data/FileManager.ts","symbol":"saveFiles","line":92,"explanation":"Generic FileManager tracker used regardless of storage backend. It walks the supplied elements, picks initialized image elements whose BinaryFileData exists and whose file version is neither in savedFiles nor savingFiles (isFileSavedOrBeingSaved), adds them to addedFiles, marks savingFiles with that version, awaits the injected this._saveFiles backend callback, records returned savedFiles and erroredFiles_save by version, and always clears savingFiles in finally. It has no Firebase-specific knowledge; Collab injects the room callback."},{"path":"excalidraw-app/data/FileManager.ts","symbol":"encodeFilesForUpload","line":228,"explanation":"App-side helper that prepares each picture for storage. For every file it UTF-8 encodes fileData.dataURL into a buffer, calls compressData with the room encryption key and metadata {id, mimeType, created, lastRetrieved}, and collects {id, buffer: encodedFile}. It then throws the fileTooBig error if buffer.byteLength exceeds maxBytes. The size check is therefore after the encrypted envelope is built, and it measures the raw data-URI string bytes, not the compressed/encrypted envelope size."},{"path":"packages/excalidraw/data/encode.ts","symbol":"compressData","line":322,"explanation":"Shared editor-package envelope builder. It creates a JSON FileEncodingInfo header (version 2, compression pako@1, encryption AES-GCM), JSON-encodes the supplied metadata, concatenates metadata plus payload with concatBuffers, deflates and AES-GCM encrypts that inner bundle using encryptData with a fresh IV, then returns concatBuffers(encoding header, IV, encrypted inner bundle). This is the binary format both the app uploader and downloader use."},{"path":"excalidraw-app/data/firebase.ts","symbol":"saveFilesToFirebase","line":144,"explanation":"Cloud bucket writer. It first awaits the lazily loaded Firebase storage SDK outside any per-file try/catch, then uploads every envelope in parallel via Promise.all to `${prefix}/${id}` with cacheControl public and a one-year max-age. Each upload has its own try/catch, so individual failures are pushed to erroredFiles while successes are pushed to savedFiles, and the id arrays are returned."},{"path":"excalidraw-app/data/FileManager.ts","symbol":"shouldUpdateImageElementStatus","line":202,"explanation":"Predicate used after an upload batch. It returns true only for an initialized image element whose fileId is present in FileManager.savedFiles and whose element.status is still 'pending'. Portal.queueFileUpload uses this to mutate the element to status 'saved', which is the signal broadcast to peers that the persisted bytes may now be fetched. Because savedFiles is populated only after backend success, an upload in progress or failed cannot flip the status early."},{"path":"excalidraw-app/collab/Collab.tsx","symbol":"fetchImageFilesFromFirebase","line":441,"explanation":"Bob's room-side selector. It filters initialized, non-deleted image elements, excluding any file already tracked by FileManager (fetching, saving, saved, or errored), then in normal mode keeps only status === 'saved'. With forceFetchFiles it uses looser criteria: any non-pending status, or a pending element whose updated timestamp is older than 10 seconds. It maps the chosen elements to fileIds and delegates to fileManager.getFiles."},{"path":"excalidraw-app/data/firebase.ts","symbol":"loadFilesFromFirebase","line":277,"explanation":"Downloader used by Bob. For each unique file id it constructs the bucket's public Firebase Storage URL ending in `%2F${id}`, fetches it over plain HTTP with `?alt=media`, checks for a status below 400, reads the arrayBuffer, runs decompressData with the room key, decodes the recovered payload into a data URI, and rebuilds BinaryFileData using metadata.mimeType and the created/lastRetrieved timestamps. Non-2xx responses and thrown errors are recorded in erroredFiles."},{"path":"packages/excalidraw/components/App.tsx","symbol":"addMissingFiles","line":4935,"explanation":"Private editor-core merge helper behind the public addFiles. It copies the current this.files map (or starts empty when replace is true), skips any file whose id is already present, and otherwise inserts the file into addedFiles and nextFiles. For SVG payloads it normalizes the data URL; if normalization changes it, it increments fileData.version from its current value (default 1) and stores the normalized URL so persistence can update the store. It then assigns this.files and returns addedFiles. The public addFiles wrapper additionally clears image shape caches, triggers a scene update, and rebuilds image cache entries."}],"flow":"Alice drops a photo: the editor inserts an image element whose file status is 'pending' and stores the BinaryFileData in the editor's binary map. When the scene broadcasts, Portal.queueFileUpload (throttled) calls FileManager.saveFiles. saveFiles walks the canvas, selects initialized image files whose version is not already saved or in flight, marks them saving, and invokes Collab's injected save callback. That callback uses encodeFilesForUpload, which builds each file's encrypted envelope through the shared compressData routine (JSON header + IV + deflated, encrypted metadata/payload bundle), then checks the size limit. The limit is checked AFTER the envelope is built and against buffer.byteLength, i.e. the UTF-8 length of the original data-URI string, not the compressed/encrypted output or the decoded image bytes; the cap is FILE_UPLOAD_MAX_BYTES = 4 MiB. saveFilesToFirebase then uploads all envelopes in parallel to the bucket with public one-year cache headers and returns succeeded/failed id lists. FileManager records those outcomes by file version. Only then does shouldUpdateImageElementStatus allow a pending element to become 'saved', and the saved status is broadcast, telling peers the bytes are ready.

On Bob's side, a received scene update calls loadImageFiles, which calls fetchImageFilesFromFirebase. In normal operation Bob downloads nothing early because the selector requires status === 'saved', the element must be an initialized, non-deleted image, and FileManager must not already be tracking that file. Since Alice only flips to 'saved' after the backend reports success, Bob cannot fetch before the upload finishes, and fetching/saving maps prevent duplicate requests. On a fresh join, App.tsx invokes the selector with forceFetchFiles: true; the looser criteria accept any non-pending status (including 'saved' or 'error') or pending elements older than 10 seconds, while still excluding deleted and already-tracked files. The selected ids go through FileManager.getFiles to loadFilesFromFirebase, which fetches the public bucket URL over plain HTTP, decrypts/inflates via decompressData, and reconstructs data URIs. Back in the editor, public addFiles calls the private addMissingFiles to merge payloads into this.files, then invalidates image shape caches and rebuilds image cache entries; updateStaleImageStatuses marks failed downloads as 'error'. If a payload id already exists in Bob's editor, addMissingFiles simply skips it, so the existing in-memory data is preserved and the newly downloaded copy is not installed. A vector/SVG payload's revision can change on arrival because, for newly added SVG files, addMissingFiles normalizes the data URL and, when normalization changes it, increments fileData.version and replaces the URL so the persistence layer treats it as a new revision.

Failure handling differs by stage: if the storage SDK itself cannot load, saveFilesToFirebase throws before entering its per-file Promise.all, so the entire save rejects with no per-file saved/errored result; queueFileUpload surfaces the error rather than silently recording individual failed ids. If only one uploadBytes call fails, it is caught inside Promise.all, that id lands in erroredFiles while other ids can land in savedFiles, the save call still resolves, and FileManager records the per-file save error."}

{
  "findings": [
    {
      "path": "packages/excalidraw/components/App.tsx",
      "symbol": "initializeScene",
      "line": 3568,
      "explanation": "The editor component's own startup routine. It awaits initialData (whether supplied directly or as a function), restores elements with restoreElements({ repairBindings: true, deleteInvisibleElements: true }) and app state with restoreAppState, resolves the initial viewport, then calls resetStore and resetHistory to clear undo/history state. It applies the restored scene through syncActionResult with CaptureUpdateAction.NEVER and clears the image shape cache. Finally, it starts font loading with this.fonts.loadSceneFonts(restoredAppState.currentItemFontFamily), deliberately reading the restored state rather than this.state because syncActionResult updates state asynchronously, and forwards the resolved faces to this.fonts.onLoaded."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/excalidraw/fonts/Fonts.ts",
      "symbol": "loadSceneFonts",
      "line": 164,
      "explanation": "Per-editor-instance warm-up for scene fonts. It reads non-deleted scene elements, obtains unique families and per-family character sets, and accepts an optional defaultFontFamily (defaulting to DEFAULT_FONT_FAMILY, Excalifont). If the supplied default is not already among the scene families, it appends it and seeds its character set with the fixed sample string 'Excalidraw', purely to preload the default face before the first text element is rendered. It then calls the static loadFontFaces with this instance's ownerDocument.fonts."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/excalidraw/fonts/Fonts.ts",
      "symbol": "getCharsPerFamily",
      "line": 484,
      "explanation": "Collects a Record keyed by numeric fontFamily, each value a Set of glyph characters used by that typeface. It only considers text elements and iterates element.originalText, the source/unwrapped text, rather than element.text, so font loading and subsetting are based on the original characters rather than the currently wrapped/displayed string. Characters are accumulated per element.fontFamily; non-text elements are skipped."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/excalidraw/fonts/Fonts.ts",
      "symbol": "loadFontFaces",
      "line": 266,
      "explanation": "Static loading routine. It first walks every registered font family and adds each font face to the document's FontFaceSet if absent, but skips families whose metadata.local is true. In this checkout those are Helvetica and the 'Segoe UI Emoji' Windows emoji fallback, so their faces are never added to the face set; all other registered faces, including Xiaolai and Liberation Sans, are added. It then creates the fontFacesLoader generator, runs it through new PromisePool(iterator, 10).all() to cap concurrency at ten load operations, and returns the pooled results flattened and filtered to remove falsy/undefined entries."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/common/src/promise-pool.ts",
      "symbol": "all",
      "line": 30,
      "explanation": "Generic bounded-concurrency collector used by both font-loading paths. It attaches a 'fulfilled' listener to the underlying es6-promise-pool, receives each result as an [index, value] tuple, and stores value in this.entries[index]. This preserves the original generator order regardless of completion order. When the pool finishes it removes the listener and returns Object.values(this.entries); undefined/void results are ignored, so failed or skipped tasks leave no entry."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/excalidraw/fonts/Fonts.ts",
      "symbol": "onLoaded",
      "line": 115,
      "explanation": "Callback invoked after fonts have loaded to force re-layout/repaint of text. For each FontFace it builds a deduplication signature from family, style, weight, and unicodeRange, and always records that signature in the static Fonts.loadedFontsCache. If the signature is not already in this instance's loadedFonts set, it adds it and marks that a real change occurred. If every signature was already processed by this instance (or the input is empty), it returns early after the static cache has still been updated. Otherwise it walks all non-deleted elements: for each text element it deletes the element from ShapeCache, clears charWidth's wrapping/width cache for that element's font string, deletes the container element from ShapeCache if present, and finally calls scene.triggerUpdate() once if any text element was invalidated."
    }
  ],
  "flow": "On mount, App.initializeScene loads and sanitizes initialData, restores elements and app state, resets the store/history, applies the restored scene via syncActionResult, and then starts font loading with the restored app state's currentItemFontFamily. loadSceneFonts collects the scene's families and characters via getCharsPerFamily, which uses originalText; it prewarms the default family only when that family is not already in the scene, assigning it the literal sample characters 'Excalidraw' at that moment. It then calls loadFontFaces, which registers every non-local face in the FontFaceSet, skips local metadata faces (Helvetica and the Segoe UI Emoji fallback), and runs fontFacesLoader through PromisePool.all with concurrency 10. For each family fontFacesLoader constructs a font string at FONT_SIZES.sm and checks fontFaceSet.check(font, text). If the check is true, the typeface is skipped without yielding a load task, so no fetch occurs because the needed faces are already available. If false, it yields a task that calls fontFaceSet.load(font, text); a rejection in that task is caught, logged with the font and registered URLs, and becomes undefined, so one failing family does not abort the rest. PromisePool.all collects fulfilled [index, value] tuples by their original index; loadFontFaces flattens and filters the results and returns the FontFace array to initializeScene, whose .then calls onLoaded. onLoaded records every signature in the static loadedFontsCache and bails early if this instance has already processed all of them, but the static cache is still updated. For genuinely new faces it invalidates all text elements' ShapeCache and glyph-width cache, invalidates their containers, and triggers a scene repaint, replacing the fallback-glyph layout with the real glyphs. Besides startup, onLoaded is invoked by the ownerDocument.fonts 'loadingdone' event, by the Safari paste/duplicate path after loadElementsFonts resolves, and by the changeFontFamily action after ownerDocument.fonts.load resolves."
}

{
  "findings": [
    {
      "path": "packages/element/src/linearElementEditor.ts",
      "symbol": "handlePointDragging",
      "line": 469,
      "explanation": "Static editor handler for dragging selected linear-element vertices. For elbow/orthogonal arrows it first rewrites any selected interior point index to the last vertex and updates lastClickedPoint. It then computes the drag delta either with _getShiftLockedDelta when Shift discrete-angle locking applies to a single point (using the effective grid unless Ctrl/Cmd is held), or with createPointAt and grid snapping. It calls pointDraggingUpdates, applies the resulting positions and binding updates with LinearElementEditor.movePoints, lifts the arrow above the hovered bindable with moveArrowAboveBindable, resizes any bound text, and, when the other end's bindable is not the current suggested target, records initialState.altFocusPoint once via projectFixedPointOntoDiagonal."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/element/src/linearElementEditor.ts",
      "symbol": "pointDraggingUpdates",
      "line": 2402,
      "explanation": "Module-private computation that converts a vertex-drag into positions plus binding updates. It asks getBindingStrategyForDraggingBindingElementEndpoints about both ends, then turns each answer into either a binding record (elementId, mode, and fixedPoint from calculateFixedPointForNonElbowArrowBinding), a null detachment, or an unchanged result when the strategy is undefined. It also derives suggestedBinding for the highlight when the dragged end is orbit-bound (or when COMPLEX_BINDINGS is off). Finally it constructs a simulated nextArrow with the dragged endpoints offset and the updated bindings, calls updateBoundPoint for both attached ends to re-seat them, and returns the final positions, updates, and hit element."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/element/src/binding.ts",
      "symbol": "getBindingStrategyForDraggingBindingElementEndpoints_simple",
      "line": 656,
      "explanation": "The default per-end binding policy used when COMPLEX_BINDINGS is false. It keeps a binding with mode undefined, detaches with mode null, attaches on the outline with mode orbit, and attaches inside with mode inside. It detaches both ends if both are dragged, detaches a dragged end if binding is disabled, delegates elbow arrows to bindingStrategyForElbowArrowEndpointDragging, handles the special new-arrow case, and handles both ends landing on the same shape as inside bindings. With Alt it short-circuits to inside mode at the pointer (or null if there is no hit). Normally, a point inside the hit shape becomes inside; otherwise it becomes orbit with the focus point obtained from projectFixedPointOntoDiagonal, falling back to the raw global point."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/element/src/collision.ts",
      "symbol": "getHoveredElementForBinding",
      "line": 460,
      "explanation": "Selector for the single binding target. It obtains candidates from getBindingCandidates, sorts them by Math.abs(distance) so the smallest absolute outline gap wins, and then considers smaller overlapping candidates that contain the point. The overlap test only accepts positive-distance (containing) candidates, computes the overlap fraction and the candidate's area relative to the closest element, and requires overlapPercent > 0.25 and relativeArea < 0.75. If the closest candidate contains the point and such a smaller overlapping shape exists, that smaller shape wins; otherwise the closest-outline candidate is returned."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/element/src/collision.ts",
      "symbol": "getBindingCandidates",
      "line": 382,
      "explanation": "Front-to-back scan over the ordered elements (end of the array toward the beginning). It skips non-bindable elements and elements clipped by an enclosing frame, keeps track of an occluding frame so only that frame's children are considered behind it, and uses bindableElementBorderDistanceIfClose to filter by reach. Locked elements are not pushed into the candidate list but can still occlude, and the scan stops after the first opaque element that contains the point (distance >= 0), because everything behind it is hidden from binding."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/element/src/collision.ts",
      "symbol": "bindableElementBorderDistanceIfClose",
      "line": 307,
      "explanation": "Signed proximity measure used for binding candidates. It first does a cheap bounds intersection with the tolerance; if the bounds miss, it returns -Infinity. A location inside a frame-like element also returns -Infinity because frames are bindable only from outside. Otherwise it computes distanceToElement: positive when the point is inside the element, and, when outside, returns the negated gap only if that gap is within tolerance; beyond tolerance it returns -Infinity."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/element/src/utils.ts",
      "symbol": "projectFixedPointOntoDiagonal",
      "line": 810,
      "explanation": "Outline-aiming helper. If midpoint snapping is enabled it first tries getSnapOutlineMidPoint and returns an edge midpoint when one is close. It rejects arrows too small to have a meaningful direction, obtains the shape's two corner-to-corner axes (diagonals or center lines), and anchors a ray at the neighbouring vertex, or for a two-point arrow at the other end's stored attachment focus point. The ray passes through the location and is intersected with both axes; the nearer crossing to the anchor is chosen when both exist, otherwise the available crossing, and the result is returned only if it lies inside the element."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/element/src/binding.ts",
      "symbol": "calculateFixedPointForNonElbowArrowBinding",
      "line": 2322,
      "explanation": "Converts a scene location (the supplied focusPoint, or the current endpoint) into the proportional fixedPoint stored on the binding record. It takes the element centre and rotates the point by the negative element angle to undo rotation. For degenerate point-like shapes smaller than MIN_BINDABLE_SIZE it returns the normalized centre [0.5, 0.5]. Otherwise it uses getBindingGap as a floor for each divisor and computes (unrotated point - element origin) / max(dimension, sizeFloor), normalizing the result, so the attachment stays proportional without exploding for tiny shapes."
    }
  ],
  "flow": "Dragging the end knob enters handlePointDragging. That handler computes the Shift-locked or grid-snapped delta, asks pointDraggingUpdates for the new positions and bindings, applies them with movePoints, and lifts the arrow above the hovered bindable. pointDraggingUpdates consults the default simple binding policy for the dragged end (and the other end). The policy asks getHoveredElementForBinding, which repeatedly uses getBindingCandidates and bindableElementBorderDistanceIfClose to gather nearby attachable shapes and choose the smallest absolute outline gap. In this scene the smaller filled card is above the large rectangle, so the front-to-back scan reaches the card first; because the card is filled it is opaque for binding, and because the knob is over it its signed distance is positive, so getBindingCandidates breaks immediately. The rectangle behind it is therefore never gathered. Its own lack of fill also means it would not be an opaque blocker if it were reached, but the card's opacity already guarantees the rectangle is not considered. The highlight is the card entry built by pointDraggingUpdates from the policy result. Just outside the card's outline, within binding tolerance, the card is still the candidate with the smallest absolute gap; the rectangle may be a containing positive-distance candidate, but its low overlap fraction and much larger relative area fail getHoveredElementForBinding's overlap rescue, so the card remains the target. Since the point is not inside the card, the simple policy returns mode 'orbit' (attach-on-outline), and its focus point is projectFixedPointOntoDiagonal's outline aim, normally an edge midpoint snap or the nearer corner-axis crossing inside the card, with the raw pointer as fallback. That focus is then stored proportionally by calculateFixedPointForNonElbowArrowBinding. If the other end is attached, the one-time altFocusPoint and the simulated nextArrow with updateBoundPoint keep that other end re-seated on its own shape. Holding Alt changes the simple policy's result from orbit/outline attachment to mode 'inside', using the pointer location as the focus and bypassing the normal point-inside check; if there is no hovered shape, Alt instead produces a detach."
}

{
  "findings": [
    {
      "path": "packages/excalidraw/scene/export.ts",
      "symbol": "exportToSvg",
      "line": 969,
      "explanation": "The vector document builder. It creates the SVG root with version/xmlns/viewBox/width/height, appends the svg-source comment, a <metadata> node, and a <defs> node. When config.embedScene is set it serializes the scene and calls encodeSvgBase64Payload inside a try/catch that only logs, so embedding failures never abort the SVG export. It then registers a <clipPath> in <defs> for each frame/artboard, inlines generated font-face declarations into a <style>, paints the background rectangle when the background is not transparent, and finally renders all shapes via renderSceneToSvg/renderElementToSvg."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/excalidraw/scene/export.ts",
      "symbol": "encodeSvgBase64Payload",
      "line": 1194,
      "explanation": "Writes the scene payload into the SVG metadata node. It calls encode({ text: payload }) to obtain the versioned byte-string record, JSON.stringify's that record, converts it to base64 with stringToBase64(..., true) because the string is already a binary/byte string, and appends comments payload-type:application/vnd.excalidraw+json, payload-version:2, payload-start, then the base64 text node, then payload-end."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/excalidraw/data/encode.ts",
